Data Types
**********

<div class=perex>

A schema is made of elements, each describing one piece of the data. This page goes through all of them:

- simple types, formats and ranges
- default values, required and nullable items
- arrays, lists, enumerations and enums
- structures and tuples
- descriptions and deprecated items

</div>

The examples continue the configuration of an application from the [overview |@home]. `$processor` is an instance of `Nette\Schema\Processor`.


Simple Types: type()
====================

All standard PHP data types can be specified in the schema:

```php
Expect::string($default = null);
Expect::int($default = null);
Expect::float($default = null);
Expect::bool($default = null);
Expect::null();
Expect::array($default = []);
Expect::list($default = []);
```

And also all types [supported by the Validators class |utils:validators#Expected Types], for example `Expect::type('scalar')` or shorthand `Expect::scalar()`. Also class or interface names, e.g., `Expect::type('AddressEntity')`.

Union syntax can also be used:

```php
Expect::type('bool|string|array')
```

The default value is always `null` with the exception of `array` and `list`, where it is an empty array. (A list is an array indexed by a sequence of numeric keys starting from zero, i.e. a non-associative array).

In version 1.x, the type `'?int[]'` means an array whose items are integers or `null`. Version 2.0 reads it the way phpDoc does, as an array of integers, or `null` in place of the whole array. If you mean an array whose items may be `null`, write `Expect::arrayOf(Expect::int()->nullable())`, which means the same in both versions.


Default, Required and Nullable
==============================

Every item that is missing in the input gets its default value. For most types it is `null`, and you set another one as the parameter or with `default()`:

```php
Expect::int(3306);
Expect::int()->default(3306); // the same
```

A default value of `null` does not mean that the input may contain `null`. `Expect::string()` accepts only a string, so `'password' => null` in the input is an error. To allow `null`, call `nullable()`:

```php
$schema = Expect::structure([
	'user' => Expect::string(),
	'password' => Expect::string()->nullable(),
]);

$processor->process($schema, ['user' => null]);
// ERROR: The item 'user' expects to be string, null given.

$processor->process($schema, ['password' => null]);
// OK, returns {'password' => null, 'user' => null}
```

An item that must be given is marked with `required()`. When it is missing, the processing reports an error instead of using the default value:

```php
$schema = Expect::structure([
	'user' => Expect::string()->required(),
]);

$processor->process($schema, []);
// ERROR: The mandatory item 'user' is missing.
```


Ranges: min() max()
===================

Use `min()` and `max()` to limit the count for arrays:

```php
// array, at least 1 item, maximum 5 items
Expect::listOf('email')->min(1)->max(5);
```

For strings, they limit the length:

```php
// string, at least 8 bytes long, maximum 64 bytes
Expect::string()->min(8)->max(64);
```

The length of a string is measured in bytes. To count characters of a UTF-8 string, use `Expect::unicode()`.

For numbers, they limit the value:

```php
// integer, between 1 and 65535 inclusive
Expect::int()->min(1)->max(65535);
```

Of course, it is possible to specify just `min()` or just `max()`:

```php
// string, maximum 64 bytes
Expect::string()->max(64);
```

On a union such as `Expect::type('int|string')`, it is not clear whether `min()` and `max()` should limit the number or the length of the string, so they raise an `E_USER_DEPRECATED` notice there. Give the limits to each variant of [anyOf() |#Enumeration: anyOf()] instead: .{data-version:1.4.0}

```php
Expect::anyOf(Expect::int()->min(1), Expect::string()->min(1));
```


Regular Expressions: pattern()
==============================

Using `pattern()`, you can specify a regular expression that the **entire** input string must match (i.e. as if it were wrapped in `^` and `$` characters):

```php
// exactly 9 digits
Expect::string()->pattern('\d{9}');
```

A pattern only makes sense for a string. That is why `Expect::string()` returns a `StringType` element, while `Expect::int()` and `Expect::float()` return a `NumberType` and `Expect::listOf()` and the other array schemas an `ArrayType`, each offering the methods of its kind. Calling `pattern()` on a number or an array raises an `E_USER_DEPRECATED` notice, and your editor flags such a call as deprecated as soon as you write it. .{data-version:1.4.0}


Arrays and Lists: arrayOf() listOf()
====================================

An array represents too general a structure; it's more useful to specify precisely which elements it may contain. For example, an array whose elements can only be strings:

```php
$schema = Expect::arrayOf('string');

$processor->process($schema, ['hello', 'world']); // OK
$processor->process($schema, ['a' => 'hello', 'b' => 'world']); // OK
$processor->process($schema, ['key' => 123]); // ERROR: 123 is not a string
```

The second parameter can specify keys (since version 1.2):

```php
$schema = Expect::arrayOf('string', 'int');

$processor->process($schema, ['hello', 'world']); // OK
$processor->process($schema, ['a' => 'hello']); // ERROR: 'a' is not an int
```

A list is an indexed array:

```php
$schema = Expect::listOf('email');

$processor->process($schema, ['jim@example.com', 'ann@example.com']); // OK
$processor->process($schema, ['jim@example.com', 'jim']); // ERROR: 'jim' is not an e-mail
$processor->process($schema, ['key' => 'jim@example.com']); // ERROR: not a list
```

With the `wrap` parameter, a single value is also accepted and wrapped into a one-item list. A configuration with a single administrator then does not need the brackets: .{data-version:1.4.0}

```php
$schema = Expect::listOf('email', wrap: true);

$processor->process($schema, 'jim@example.com'); // OK, gives ['jim@example.com']
```

The parameter can also be a schema, so we can write:

```php
Expect::arrayOf(Expect::bool())
```

The default value is an empty array. If you specify a default value, it will be merged with the passed data. This can be disabled using `mergeDefaults(false)` (since version 1.1).


Enumeration: anyOf()
====================

`anyOf()` represents a set of values or schemas that a value can take. Here's how to write an array of elements that can be either `'a'`, `true`, or `null`:

```php
$schema = Expect::listOf(
	Expect::anyOf('a', true, null),
);

$processor->process($schema, ['a', true, null, 'a']); // OK
$processor->process($schema, ['a', false]); // ERROR: false does not belong there
```

The elements of the enumeration can also be schemas:

```php
$schema = Expect::listOf(
	Expect::anyOf(Expect::string(), true, null),
);

$processor->process($schema, ['foo', true, null, 'bar']); // OK
$processor->process($schema, [123]); // ERROR
```

The `anyOf()` method accepts variants as separate parameters, not as an array. To pass it an array of values, use the unpack operator `anyOf(...$variants)`.

Variants are tried in the order they are written, and the first one that matches wins. The only exception is `null`: when the set accepts it, a `null` input always gives `null`, even if a structure or array variant listed earlier would otherwise turn it into an empty structure or array.

The default value is `null`. Use the `firstIsDefault()` method to make the first item the default:

```php
// default is 'hello'
Expect::anyOf(Expect::string('hello'), true, null)->firstIsDefault();
```


Enums: enum() .{data-version:1.4.0}
===================================

A configuration file can only hold a plain string such as `'production'`, but in your code you would much rather work with an enum. `enum()` converts one into the other: give it a backed enum, i.e. one whose cases have values (`enum Mode: string`), and it accepts either the value or the case itself, and always gives you back the case:

```php
enum Mode: string
{
	case Development = 'development';
	case Production = 'production';
}

$schema = Expect::enum(Mode::class);

$processor->process($schema, 'production');      // OK, returns Mode::Production
$processor->process($schema, Mode::Development); // OK, returns Mode::Development
$processor->process($schema, 'test');            // ERROR: expects to be Mode
```

Enums backed by integers work exactly the same, and so does everything you know from the other elements, such as `Expect::enum(Mode::class)->required()` or `->default(Mode::Production)`.

When [generating a schema from a class |normalization#Mapping to Objects: from()], a `public Mode $mode` property gives you this very element, so you do not have to reach for `enum()` there. `Expect::type(Mode::class)` is a different thing: like any class name, it accepts only an instance, i.e. the case itself.


Structures
==========

Structures are objects with defined keys. Each key-value pair is referred to as a "property".

Structures accept arrays and objects and return `stdClass` objects. To get an array instead, add `castTo('array')`.

By default, all properties are optional and have a default value of `null`. You can define mandatory properties using `required()`:

```php
$schema = Expect::structure([
	'user' => Expect::string()->required(),
	'password' => Expect::string(), // default value is null
]);

$processor->process($schema, ['password' => 'secret']);
// ERROR: The mandatory item 'user' is missing.

$processor->process($schema, ['user' => 'admin']);
// OK, returns {'user' => 'admin', 'password' => null}
```

A structure itself is mandatory. Therefore, if it is nested inside another structure and the input does not contain it, it is created anyway - and it reports an error when it contains a required property. Use `required(false)` to make the entire nested structure optional. If it is missing in the input, `null` appears in the output, but if it is present, its required properties are enforced:

```php
$schema = Expect::structure([
	'database' => Expect::structure([
		'user' => Expect::string()->required(),
	])->required(false),
]);

$processor->process($schema, []);
// OK, returns {'database' => null}

$processor->process($schema, ['database' => []]);
// ERROR: The mandatory item 'database › user' is missing.
```

If you do not want properties with default values in the output, use `skipDefaults()`:

```php
$schema = Expect::structure([
	'user' => Expect::string()->required(),
	'password' => Expect::string(),
])->skipDefaults();

$processor->process($schema, ['user' => 'admin']);
// OK, returns {'user' => 'admin'}
```

The array of all structure properties is returned by the `getShape()` method.

By default, no additional items can be present in the input data. That is how a misspelled key is caught:

```php
$schema = Expect::structure([
	'host' => Expect::string(),
]);

$processor->process($schema, ['hots' => 'localhost']);
// ERROR: Unexpected item 'hots', did you mean 'host'?
```

This can be changed using `otherItems()`. As a parameter, pass the schema to validate each extra item:

```php
$schema = Expect::structure([
	'host' => Expect::string(),
])->otherItems(Expect::int());

$processor->process($schema, ['timeout' => 30]); // OK
$processor->process($schema, ['timeout' => true]); // ERROR
```

You can create a new structure by extending another using `extend()`:

```php
$database = Expect::structure([
	'host' => Expect::string('localhost'),
	'port' => Expect::int(3306),
]);

$replica = $database->extend([
	'readOnly' => Expect::bool(true),
]);
```


Array .{data-version:1.3.2}
===========================

An array with defined keys. Everything that applies to [#structures] applies to it.

```php
$schema = Expect::array([
	'user' => Expect::string()->required(),
	'password' => Expect::string(), // default value is null
]);
```


Tuples: tuple() .{data-version:1.4.0}
=====================================

A tuple is a fixed-size array where each position has its own schema:

```php
$schema = Expect::tuple([
	Expect::int(),
	Expect::string(),
	Expect::bool(),
]);

$processor->process($schema, [1, 'hello', true]); // OK
```

The input must be a list, so positions given by keys or in a different order are refused. When the input is shorter, the missing positions behave like missing items of a structure: they get their default value, or report an error when they are `required()`. `otherItems()` lets further items follow the defined positions and sets the schema they must match, while `extend()` adds more positions to the end.

When configurations are layered, a later tuple replaces the earlier one as a whole, because mixing the positions of two different tuples would only produce nonsense.

Unlike a structure, a tuple does not accept `null` as an empty value; call `nullable()` when `null` is a valid value.


Deprecated Items: deprecated()
==============================

When you rename or drop a configuration option, the old one can keep working for a while and warn its users. Mark it with `deprecated([string $message])`. The warnings are returned by `$processor->getWarnings()`:

```php
$schema = Expect::structure([
	'server' => Expect::string()->deprecated('The item %path% is deprecated, use host'),
	'host' => Expect::string(),
]);

$processor->process($schema, ['server' => 'localhost']); // OK
$processor->getWarnings(); // ["The item 'server' is deprecated, use host"]
```


Description: description() .{data-version:1.4.0}
================================================

Any element can carry a human-readable description. It has no effect on validation, but the schema is often the only place where the meaning of a configuration option is written down at all, and `description()` lets you write it right there. The description also travels into the [JSON Schema export |json-schema]:

```php
$schema = Expect::structure([
	'host' => Expect::string('localhost')->description('Database server hostname'),
	'port' => Expect::int(3306)->description('Port the server listens on'),
])->description('Database connection');
```

